A: If the return value of the Contains() method is false, it does not mean that any previous value of indexOf(x) or lastIndexOf(x) will be updated; instead it simply means they would return another value if the current value is present. But, in the worst case scenario, it might yield another value -1 (indicating the presence of x), or a value equal to or greater than the count of the list minus 1, depending upon whether x is greater than the last item in the list or not, respectively.
Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX); this line is not entirely correct. It does not prove that if Contains() returned false, the IndexOf() would not have been updated. It just checks if the old value was not greater than the new value, which might not be a correct indicator. 
The PexAssert is equivalent to Assert.IsTrue for arrays in Pex, so your PexAssert for the following line will never fire since PexAssert does not accept more than one argument. 
Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListLastIndexOfX); is also somewhat erroneous; the same comment applies as for the previous point. 
Finally, Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount); won't fire for a Contains() test case with false return because the counts do not always change. The count of the array might be incremented but that would not mean the presence of 'x' in the array is guaranteed. If 'x' is not present, the count should not change.
Also, it's worth mentioning the behavior of ArrayList.Contains() can be unpredictable if you are modifying the list within the loop where Contains() is being called (i.e., Add/Remove operations).
For precise verification of your test-case, you might want to consider creating an explicit contract method for it.
Hope, this makes sense...

A: Pex cannot check all possible test cases due to the limitations of automated testing, particularly the unpredictable behavior of classes like ArrayList. For more robust testing, you may consider using libraries like NUnit that offer more powerful assertion capabilities than the basic Assert class in .NET.
Unfortunately, Pex currently does not support the use of other fully-fledged test frameworks like NUnit, MSTest in conjunction with it. Pex is trying to stay simple and lightweight to make it easier to use.
A simple solution is for you to look at the implementation of ArrayList.Contains and modify your test case accordingly. For example, if you find the ArrayList.Contains method checks for an item in the ArrayList by iterating over the ArrayList, and in worst case scenario it iterates over the entire ArrayList, then you can modify your test case to match this behavior.
Again, this is not a perfect solution and you should use NUnit or any other fully-featured test framework for more precise testing. 
Moreover, please note that automated unit testing has its limits and sometimes, we have to rely on manual testing to get 100% coverage.
Also, you can consider writing Pex 'hooks' for any operations you might want Pex to understand and take it into account during testing. However, if the class you're interacting with behaves in such a way that you cannot determine the correct outcomes, it makes Pex really difficult and you might end up with a lot of non-deterministic failures.
Let me know if you need further assistance.

A: The key here is to realize that the Contains method can change the internal state of the ArrayList instance. This is not just about counting, which is not altered when items are present or not, but about the order and indices.
Imagine you have an ArrayList of numbers: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}.
If you execute Contains and the--------------------            if (New_Ret == false)
	        {
                if (Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != -1)
	            {
    		        Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	            }
                else if (New_arrListIndexOfX != -1 && New_arrListLastIndexOfX != -1)
	            {
    		        Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	            }
	        }

        }
    }
}

```

This code verifies the following:

* Given `arrList` and `x` as input, the return value of `arrList.Contains(x)` should be `False` if and only if neither `IndexOf(x)` nor `LastIndexOf(x)` have returned indices within the valid range (i.e., within the range [0, count - 1]).
* `arrList.Count` should not have changed after this operation for `arrList.Contains(x)` when the return value is `False`.
  * Note: For `.Net`, `System.Collections.ArrayList.Count` does not guarantee any specific value after adding or removing items. Therefore, this assertion is not always true. This may or may not be expected behavior.
* `IndexOf(x)` and `LastIndexOf(x)` should return either -1 (which is not a valid index) or the same value when the return value of `Contains(x)` is `False`. 

This implies that you can't have both valid `IndexOf(x)` and `LastIndexOf(x)` at once if the return value is `False`. An object cannot be present in the sequence `arrList` more than once.

### What you should avoid to do

Although this gives you quite a good assurance about what ArrayList.Contains() is supposed to do, the assertions are not applicable to all code paths.

For example, if the index of x is within the valid range, but no more than one index after the previous value of `LastIndexOf(x)`, then `LastIndexOf(x)` will still return a valid value (even though `IndexOf(x)` is not at the previous valid index + 1). Thus, the assertions will be wrong in this case, but the method still correctly behaves for all other valid inputs.

There are other possible code paths that might not be covered by these assertions.
Therefore, you should only use this contract check as an aid for verification and it can't replace understanding of the specification.

Also, Pex does not support conditional/branching code that might not be reached given the inputs. Therefore, if the check is put on a branch like if/else, assertions on true and false branches are not supported.  

It's better to understand the specification of your method thoroughly before using this assertion. 


