I am new to Pex and I just want to run this test in the context of the existing ArrayList methods and not in isolation. It seems like it's looking to test my methods which is not what I want. I guess I can write my own custom precondition to prevent this from happening.
Any idea what's going on here, and how I can get Pex to work in the context of existing methods instead of my methods?

A: Partial tests are generated in Pex based on call paths and parameters of the method under test.
Your method doesn't belong to a class, so Pex doesn't include it in the class under test. This makes the creation of tests "partial" as the focus is on individual statements, not on the system under test.
Consider moving your Pex test to a separate class and call your ArrayList methods from there. The test would then be based on the calls instead of the statements themselves.
Example of the class you can create:
[PexClass(typeof(YourArrayListImplementationClass))]
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    private YourArrayListImplementationClass _arrList = new YourArrayListImplementationClass();

    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_ContainsContract(int x)
    {
        bool New_Ret = _arrList.Contains(x);
        // ... do asserts
    }
}

In this way, the test is based on the call to ArrayList's methods. This way the test will use the existing methods (`Contains`, `IndexOf`, etc.) when it generates calls.

A: When your Pex-test methods do not belong to a class, Pex treats the test as "isolated," as if it is written in isolation, without knowing anything about the rest of your codebase. This is the reason for your Pex-test methods not being based on any existing ArrayList methods.
So it is not possible to run a test in the context of existing ArrayList methods using Pex.
However, if you still want to test your custom methods in the context of the existing ArrayList methods, you could create your own list class and inject dependencies to it, but Pex analysis will only apply into your custom methods' context.
Or another workaround can be to create your own list which derive from ArrayList, and overwrite the methods you want to test.
For example:
public class MyArrayList : ArrayList 
{ 
    public MyArrayList() : base() { } 

    public override bool Contains(object value) 
    { 
         // Custom logic here 
    }
 }

Then when you run your Pex tests on MyArrayList class, Pex will use its own logic in Contains method.
Yet another work-around could be, creating unit tests from scratch on top of your custom logic, and then check the coverage of your ArrayList functionality is covered by these tests.

A: I tried to use Pex to test the ArrayList class which already exists in .NET Framework, and I find a way to add some constraints for test which is not working as expecting. Pex is designed to work with user defined classes and objects. ArrayList class in .NET framework doesn't belong to any of our classes and it's a framework class, Pex can't run test with ArrayList using framework classes.
Pex can only work with user defined objects (classes,structs) that has to be part of a class other than a simple class. It can't work with non-user defined classes such as framework classes.
I hope this helps and will provide a good direction for testing classes that uses framework classes.
Update: Pex does not currently work with any existing .NET class libraries. It works only with classes defined by you in your code.
That's my understanding of the matter. If you can provide a bit more detail or context, I can give a more specific answer.
